---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "Jev RAG Benchmark: does TypeSafe Jev 1.13 actually improve a RAG pipeline?",
  description:
    "A free English benchmark of TypeSafe Jev 1.13 as the reranking and decision layer of a RAG pipeline — frozen candidate pools, probability calibration, paired confidence intervals, and honest negative results.",
  about: [
    "Jev 1.13",
    "Retrieval-augmented generation",
    "Reranking",
    "Model calibration",
    "Benchmarking",
  ],
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Emre Ulgac", url: "https://emretheus.xyz" },
  datePublished: "2026-09-21",
  inLanguage: "en",
  mainEntityOfPage: "https://emretheus.xyz/systems/jev-rag-benchmark/",
  codeRepository: "https://github.com/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark",
};

const decisions = [
  {
    id: "D1",
    title: "A free stack, or a more reproducible one?",
    context:
      "A benchmark nobody can afford to re-run is a blog post. Reranking evaluations burn tokens on every method you compare.",
    options: "Paid APIs · local models on a laptop · free tiers of hosted models",
    choice:
      "Free tiers only: Vercel AI Gateway for Jev 1.13, NVIDIA NIM for embeddings and the cross-encoder, Codiv for generation.",
    why:
      "Every published number can be reproduced by anyone with an account and no budget approval. The whole benchmark — 1,490 reranked queries across four methods — cost $0.",
    cost:
      "Model churn. Three NVIDIA models were retired mid-project and had to be replaced; each swap is recorded in the run manifest so results stay attributable.",
  },
  {
    id: "D2",
    title: "A RAG framework, or a harness of my own?",
    context:
      "Reranking comparisons are easy to run in LlamaIndex or LangChain — and easy to contaminate with their prompt wrappers, parsers and batching.",
    options: "Adopt a framework · build a thin harness · fork an existing benchmark",
    choice: "A framework-neutral harness with three runtime dependencies.",
    why:
      "A benchmark should measure models, not wrappers. Fewer layers also means clean latency numbers — and latency is a headline claim for System One models.",
    cost:
      "The glue has to be written and tested: BM25, reciprocal rank fusion, metrics, calibration, export. That work is the repo.",
  },
  {
    id: "D3",
    title: "How to keep the comparison honest",
    context:
      "If retrieval re-runs between methods, a lucky or unlucky draw gets published as a model difference.",
    options: "Live retrieval per method · frozen candidate pools · frozen pools plus frozen contexts",
    choice:
      "Freeze the top-20 candidates once, compare every reranker on that pool, then replay generation over one frozen top-5.",
    why:
      "Retrieval, reranking and generation effects are separated by construction. Paired bootstrap confidence intervals compare methods on identical queries.",
    cost:
      "The stages cannot adapt to each other. A real system could rerank the pool differently per query; the benchmark deliberately cannot.",
  },
  {
    id: "D4",
    title: "Publish the runs where the hypothesis loses",
    context:
      "Jev helps where the first stage leaves headroom. On XQuAD the baseline is already at 98.1% nDCG — and a trained cross-encoder still leads by 0.45 points.",
    options: "Report only the wins · report everything · report everything and label what came later",
    choice:
      "Report everything, with a preregistered protocol and an explicit changelog that marks later analyses as exploratory.",
    why:
      "A benchmark's credibility comes from the runs where its hypothesis loses. 'No measurable difference' is a finding, not a failure.",
    cost:
      "The headline is less shiny, and later additions had to carry an honest 'exploratory' label instead of being framed as confirmatory.",
  },
  {
    id: "D5",
    title: "Always-on reranking, or confidence-gated decisions?",
    context:
      "Ranking every candidate by independent probabilities is noisy. The model's own calibration offered a different interface.",
    options: "Always-on reranking · all-or-nothing switching · confidence-partitioned decisions",
    choice:
      "Keep candidates with p ≥ 0.5 in the model's order, fall back to the hybrid order for the rest; skip generation entirely when the top probability is low.",
    why:
      "The decision layer beat the unranked baseline on both datasets (+0.89 pts XQuAD, +4.14 pts SciFact), and answerability gating removed 9.8% of generation calls while raising answer F1.",
    cost:
      "A threshold must be chosen, documented and re-validated per domain. Gating is a policy, not a property of the model.",
  },
];

const rerankRows = [
  { dataset: "xquad-en", n: "1190", a: "98.11%", t: "98.93%", nModel: "99.37%" },
  { dataset: "scifact", n: "300", a: "71.67%", t: "79.29%", nModel: "78.70%" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Jev RAG Benchmark — Systems — Emretheus"
  description="Does TypeSafe Jev 1.13 actually improve a RAG pipeline? A free English benchmark with frozen candidate pools, probability calibration and paired confidence intervals — including the runs where the hypothesis lost."
  path="/systems/jev-rag-benchmark/"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label label-ember reveal">Systems · case study</p>
      <h1 class="display page-title reveal">Jev RAG Benchmark</h1>
      <p class="page-lead reveal">
        Does TypeSafe's Jev 1.13 improve a RAG pipeline? I built a free, English
        benchmark to find out — with frozen candidate pools, calibrated
        probabilities, and the runs where the hypothesis lost. This is the study
        of its decisions, not a feature tour.
      </p>
      <p class="page-meta reveal">
        <span class="label">evaluation · retrieval · LLM systems</span>
        <a
          class="label meta-link"
          href="https://github.com/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Repository ↗</a
        >
        <a
          class="label meta-link"
          href="https://github.com/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark/blob/main/docs/protocol.md"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Preregistered protocol ↗</a
        >
        <a
          class="label meta-link"
          href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark-leaderboard"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Live leaderboard ↗</a
        >
        <a
          class="label meta-link"
          href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Raw artifacts ↗</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">The question</p>
    <p class="prose-lead">
      Jev 1.13 returns typed decisions with calibrated probabilities instead of
      text — a natural fit for one of RAG's quietest jobs: deciding which
      retrieved passages deserve to reach the model, and when it is safe to
      skip a generation call entirely. The benchmark puts Jev, a trained NVIDIA
      cross-encoder and the unranked hybrid order on identical frozen
      candidates, then asks a single question: does the model actually improve
      the system?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">The constraints</p>
    <ul class="constraints">
      <li>
        <span class="label">01</span>
        <p>English data only: XQuAD-EN and SciFact — no translation confound.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="label">02</span>
        <p>$0 budget. Free tiers or the benchmark does not get re-run.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="label">03</span>
        <p>Framework-neutral. The numbers must describe models, not wrappers.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="label">04</span>
        <p>Negative results stay in. Exploratory analyses say so.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">The decisions</p>
    <ol class="decision-list">
      {
        decisions.map((d) => (
          <li class="decision reveal" data-decision={d.id}>
            <div class="decision-head">
              <span class="decision-id label">{d.id}</span>
              <h2 class="decision-title">{d.title}</h2>
            </div>
            <dl class="decision-body">
              <div><dt class="label">Context</dt><dd>{d.context}</dd></div>
              <div><dt class="label">Options</dt><dd>{d.options}</dd></div>
              <div><dt class="label">Chosen</dt><dd class="is-chosen">{d.choice}</dd></div>
              <div><dt class="label">Why</dt><dd>{d.why}</dd></div>
              <div><dt class="label">The cost</dt><dd>{d.cost}</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">The result</p>
    <p class="prose-lead">
      nDCG@10 on identical frozen top-20 candidates. A = no reranker (hybrid
      order), T = TypeSafe Jev 1.13 batch noul, N = NVIDIA cross-encoder.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll reveal">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dataset</th>
            <th>n</th>
            <th>A · no reranker</th>
            <th>T · Jev 1.13</th>
            <th>N · NVIDIA</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rerankRows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{row.dataset}</td>
                <td>{row.n}</td>
                <td>{row.a}</td>
                <td class="is-chosen">{row.t}</td>
                <td>{row.nModel}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="chart reveal">
      <img
        src="/benchmark/reranker-comparison.svg"
        alt="nDCG@10 by method with 95% confidence intervals for XQuAD-EN and SciFact"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <ul class="prose-list findings">
      <li>
        <strong>Jev improves retrieval where there is headroom.</strong> +0.82
        nDCG points over the unranked baseline on XQuAD (95% CI +0.35 to +1.31)
        and +7.62 on SciFact (CI +4.88 to +10.38).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Against a trained cross-encoder: a tie on SciFact.</strong>
        +0.59 points, CI −1.25 to +2.42 — no measurable difference — while
        NVIDIA leads XQuAD by 0.45 points. Jev is not a universal ranker.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Calibration is the sleeper feature.</strong> ECE 1.33% on XQuAD
        (6.25% on SciFact); top-1 confidence 0.96 when correct versus 0.74 when
        wrong. At p ≥ 0.7, precision is 94.9% against a 5% base rate — a 19x
        lift that a ranking metric alone would never show.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confidence-gated decisions beat the baseline everywhere.</strong>
        Partitioned reranking added +0.89 points on XQuAD (CI +0.45 to +1.36)
        and +4.14 on SciFact (CI +2.13 to +6.29); answerability gating removed
        9.8% of generation calls while raising answer F1 from 30.6% to 31.3%.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every number above cost $0.</strong> Vercel AI Gateway free tier
        for Jev, NVIDIA NIM trial for embeddings and the cross-encoder, Codiv
        for generation. At list price the Jev calls would have been $0.33.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="chart reveal">
      <img
        src="/benchmark/calibration-reliability.svg"
        alt="Reliability diagram: Jev mean confidence versus observed accuracy per probability bin"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">Bonus: tool-calling governance</p>
    <p class="prose-lead">
      Decision models can also govern tool calls: which tool to use, whether a
      call may run without review, whether arguments are valid, and whether
      observed text tries to manipulate the assistant. Same deterministic cases
      for all three: Jev through the Vercel free tier, Laya (open weights)
      through its official Hugging Face demo Space, and a free generative LLM
      asked for the same decisions as JSON.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll reveal">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Task family (n = 40)</th>
            <th>Jev 1.13</th>
            <th>Laya</th>
            <th>LLM baseline</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tool selection, 5 tools</td><td>100%</td><td>100%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tool selection, 20 tools</td><td>90%</td><td>100%</td><td>80%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tool selection, 50 tools</td><td>70%</td><td>40%</td><td>70%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tool selection, 200 tools</td><td class="is-chosen">80%</td><td>refused*</td><td>80%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Call approval</td><td>70%</td><td>32.5%</td><td class="is-chosen">80%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Argument validation</td><td>100%</td><td>65%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Injection detection</td><td>100%</td><td>100%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Median decision latency</td><td>1022 ms</td><td class="is-chosen">121 ms</td><td>1018 ms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="chart reveal">
      <img
        src="/benchmark/toolbench-cardinality.svg"
        alt="Tool selection accuracy by catalog size for Jev, Laya and an LLM baseline"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <ul class="prose-list findings">
      <li>
        <strong>Laya's 200-option result is a refusal, not a wrong answer.</strong>
        Its English checkpoint rejects the question outright:
        <code>options do not fit in head_max_len=192 tokens</code>. Degradation
        starts earlier — 40% at 50 options.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A free LLM matches Jev on accuracy here.</strong> Selection 82.5%
        vs 85%, approval 80% vs 70%, validation and injection tied at 100%. Jev's
        edge is operational: typed answers with no parsing, probabilities as a
        first-class API, many questions in one parallel request, input-only
        pricing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fairness notes.</strong> Cases are synthetic and
        template-generated (one seed); Laya was served as its English base
        checkpoint; the LLM received the same state and questions with a
        JSON-only instruction; latency includes each hosted route (Laya's own
        reported model time is ~121 ms).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">What failed</p>
    <ul class="prose-list">
      <li>
        Expecting gains everywhere. On XQuAD the baseline was already at 98.1%
        nDCG; there was almost nothing left to win, and always-on reranking
        added noise instead of value.
      </li>
      <li>
        Expecting to beat a trained cross-encoder. NVIDIA still leads XQuAD by
        0.45 points; the honest summary is "useful decision layer", not
        "best ranker".
      </li>
      <li>
        Ignoring provider lifecycle. Three NVIDIA models were retired mid-run;
        the pipeline now pins versions and records every resolved model.
      </li>
      <li>
        Expecting free-tier throughput. The gateway answered bursts with
        <code>429</code> and <code>Retry-After: 60</code>; the run settled at
        roughly one request per second and checkpointed every 100 rows.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap sec">
    <p class="label section-label">What I learned</p>
    <ul class="prose-list">
      <li>
        A benchmark's most valuable output is often the constraint map: where
        the model helps, where it does not, and what it must never be used for.
      </li>
      <li>
        For decision models, calibration is the product. Probabilities are what
        let a pipeline save money — ranking is just one consumer of them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Report latency with its conditions. Jev's 4-second median in the run is
        an artifact of free-tier pacing; a single request answered in 0.6
        seconds. Publishing one number without the other would be
        misinformation.
      </li>
      <li>
        "No measurable difference" is a real finding. A trustworthy benchmark
        needs a home for it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <nav class="wrap sec sys-next" aria-label="Systems">
    <a class="sys-next-link" href="/systems/">
      <span class="label">Back to</span>
      <span>All systems</span>
    </a>
    <p class="label">
      Reproduce it: <a
        class="meta-link"
        href="https://github.com/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/emretheus/jev-rag-benchmark ↗</a
      >
    </p>
  </nav>
</BaseLayout>

